## 2.3 Model catalog design

Implement the catalog in a dedicated module instead of scattering model IDs throughout adapter code.

Each `ModelInfo` record should support fields such as:

```ts
interface ModelInfo {
  id: string;
  provider: string;
  displayName?: string;
  description?: string;

  capabilities: {
    chat?: boolean;
    streaming?: boolean;
    tools?: boolean;
    structuredOutput?: boolean;
    vision?: boolean;
    audioInput?: boolean;
    audioOutput?: boolean;
    embeddings?: boolean;
    reranking?: boolean;
    reasoning?: boolean;
  };

  contextWindow?: number;
  maxOutputTokens?: number;
  inputModalities?: string[];
  outputModalities?: string[];

  lifecycle?: "stable" | "preview" | "deprecated" | "unknown";
  aliasOf?: string;

  sourceURL?: string;
  verifiedAt?: string;
  availability?: "hosted" | "local" | "browser" | "deployment";
}
```

Refine the schema where necessary, but keep it backward-compatible and easy to extend.

The catalog must distinguish:

- Model IDs.
- Human-readable names.
- Stable aliases.
- Versioned model IDs.
- Preview models.
- Deprecated IDs.
- Task-specific models.
- Models available only through particular deployment regions or providers.
- Model repositories from API model IDs.
- Model IDs that are valid locally but not remotely hosted.

Never assume that a Hugging Face repository ID is automatically callable from every inference provider.

Never assume that a model listed in an Ollama registry is installed on the developer's machine.

Never assume that a model supported by a browser runtime is compatible with a hosted vendor API.

